            <ArticleTitle>Isolated huge aneurysm of the left main coronary artery in a 22-year-old patient with type 1 neurofibromatosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1055-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.athoracsur.2014.05.062</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0003-4975(14)01182-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 22-year-old patient with neurofibromatosis type 1 presented with acute chest pain. A computed tomography scan and coronary angiography revealed a partially thrombosed huge aneurysm of the left main coronary artery. Despite medical treatment, the patient's angina recurred. The patient underwent a coronary bypass grafting operation and surgical exclusion of the aneurysm. Postoperative imaging disclosed good permeability of the 3 coronary artery bypass grafts and complete thrombosis of the excluded aneurysm.</AbstractText>
